izations】\nL137:       * 【137†ORT model format】\nL138:       * 【138†ORT model format runtime optimization】\nL139:     * 【139†Transformers optimizer】\nL140:     * 【140†End to end optimization with Olive】\nL141:     * 【141†Device tensors】\nL142:     * 【142†Arm® KleidiAI™ software】\nL143:   * 【143†Ecosystem】\nL144:     * 【144†Azure Container for PyTorch (ACPT)】\nL145:   * 【145†Reference】\nL146:     * 【146†Releases】\nL147:     * 【147†Compatibility】\nL148:     * 【148†Operators】\nL149:       * 【149†Operator kernels】\nL150:       * 【150†Contrib operators】\nL151:       * 【151†Custom operators】\nL152:       * 【152†Reduced operator config file】\nL153:     * 【153†Architecture】\nL154:     * 【154†Citing ONNX Runtime】\nL155:   * 【155†Dependency Management in ONNX Runtime】\nL156:   * 【156†ONNX Runtime Docs on GitHub†github.com】\nL157: \nL158: This site uses 【157†Just the Docs†github.com】, a documentation theme for Jekyll.\nL159: \nL160: [Input: Search onnxruntime] Search onnxruntime\nL161: \nL162:   * 【158†ONNX Runtime】\nL163:   * 【2†Install】\nL164:   * 【3†Get Started】\nL165:   * 【19†Tutorials】\nL166:   * 【67†API Docs】\nL167:   * 【159†YouTube†www.youtube.com】\nL168:   * 【160†GitHub†github.com】\nL169: # ONNX Runtime Execution Providers\nL170: \nL171: ONNX Runtime works with different hardware acceleration libraries through its extensible Execution Providers (EP) framework to optimally execute the ONNX models on the hardware platform. This interface enables flexibility for the AP application developer to deploy their ONNX models in different environments in the cloud and the edge and optimize the execution by taking advantage of the compute capabilities of the platform.\nL172: 【161†Image: Executing ONNX models across different HW environments†www.onnxruntime.ai】\nL173: ONNX Runtime works with the execution provider(s) using the `GetCapability()` interface to allocate specific nodes or sub-graphs for execution by the EP library in supported hardware. The EP libraries that are pre-installed in the execution environment process and execute the ONNX sub-graph on the hardware.\nL174: This architecture abstracts out the details of the hardware specific libraries that are essential to optimize the execution of deep neural networks across hardware platforms like CPU, GPU, FPGA or specialized NPUs.\nL175: 【162†Image: ONNX Runtime GetCapability()†www.onnxruntime.ai】\nL176: \nL177: ONNX Runtime supports many different execution providers today. Some of the EPs are in production for live service, while others are released in preview to enable developers to develop and customize their application using the different options.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nProfiling tools | onnxruntime (https://onnxruntime.ai/docs/performance/tune-performance/profiling-tools.html)\nciteturn80view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://onnxruntime.ai/docs/performance/tune-performance/profiling-tools.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 242\nL0: 【0†Skip to main content】\nL1:   * 【1†ONNX Runtime】\nL2:   * 【2†Install ONNX Runtime】\nL3:   * 【3†Get Started】\nL4:     * 【4†Python】\nL5:     * 【5†C++】\nL6:     * 【6†C】\nL7:     * 【7†C#】\nL8:     * 【8†Java】\nL9:     * 【9†JavaScript】\nL10:       * 【10†Web】\nL11:       * 【11†Node.js binding】\nL12:       * 【12†React Native】\nL13:     * 【13†Objective-C】\nL14:     * 【14†Julia, Ruby and Rust APIs】\nL15:     * 【15†Windows】\nL16:     * 【16†Mobile】\nL17:     * 【17†On-Device Training】\nL18:     * 【18†Large Model Training】\nL19:   * 【19†Tutorials】\nL20:     * 【20†API Basics】\nL21:     * 【21†Accelerate PyTorch】\nL22:       * 【22†PyTorch Inference】\nL23:       * 【23†Inference on multiple targets】\nL24:       * 【24†Accelerate PyTorch Training】\nL25:     * 【25†Accelerate TensorFlow】\nL26:     * 【26†Accelerate Hugging Face】\nL27:     * 【27†Deploy on AzureML】\nL28:     * 【28†Deploy on mobile】\nL29:       * 【29†Object detection and pose estimati